/ref 1031 "Integrating Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050489 #edb/ref 1033 478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050489 #edb/ref 1034 1.4445686090904644 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050489 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4d6f30e9-3dde-50ef-bfb2-830878d94eaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133374 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133374 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133374 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133374 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133374 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133374 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133374 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133374 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186133376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133374 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eea0d8dc-6a12-5e38-917e-ecb844858392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133375 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133375 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133375 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ccd4eedd-785c-50cc-b42b-d0c55d4dc7fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133376 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133376 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133376 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09ad71cc-daf3-58b7-84a2-09b46abc76fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133377 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133377 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133377 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0eb7a6d3-821c-5832-b9c1-72309e5f24de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133378 #edb/ref 1080 "32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133378 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133378 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "53a6eac6-21c2-55fa-83d0-f2462264f741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133379 #edb/ref 1080 "64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133379 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133379 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "53fcafd6-845c-5bbc-8f67-a9ab04063640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133682 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133682 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle {x(t)}, {y(t)} \\rangle   = x(0)y(0) + x(1)y(1) + x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nGiven that $p(t) = 1 + t + t^2$ and $q(t) = kt - t^2$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133682 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4eeb5c3-c1d5-5eb2-bb4e-03de915e0dab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133682 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)+p(-1)q(-1) \\\\  & =(1)(0)+(3)(k-1)+(1)(-k-1) \\\\  & =0+(3k-3)+(-k-1) \\\\  & =2k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2k-4 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133683 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8580" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133683 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x(t) = kt^2+3t$ and $y(t) = t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133683 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2d31046-ad1b-5de2-8d2b-19c2ce8b2a0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133683 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{2}+3t)(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{4}}{4}+{t}^{3}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{4}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4} & =-1 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133684 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133684 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nGiven that ![](images/7c/7c21bcd5cfd8a91b756c7d1f62b75099a7dc122b3d0460f63b860f2a5804063a.svg) and ![](images/3a/3a1cf39c7f0e8f3200bbd54ca35b67ec54bebff2d88c464ba31eaad045fbaa0f.svg) are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133684 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9ae1458-cfaf-5a3d-a1c8-d7d539480fd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133684 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $\\mathbf x$ and $\\mathbf y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot k)+1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =6k-2-4 \\\\  & =6k-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6k-6 & =0 \\\\ 6k & =6 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133685 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8582" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133685 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,2\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(t) g(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t)=\\sin 2t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = 0$\n\nII. $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\nIII. $f(t) \\perp g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity* $\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta=\\dfrac12(\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133685 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f084687c-bbe3-5506-b803-ae2c7e450a8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133685 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle  =  \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\cos{t} \\sin2t \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{3}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133866 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133867 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133867 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133867 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133867 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133867 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133867 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133867 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360808 