2186133987 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133987 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fcf94a4b-8800-5fa9-a920-52e87eab8bba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134435 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134435 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44057" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134435 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134435 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186134435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51bb8df2-4e37-58ba-9ead-0159a1efcc4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134435 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-1\\cdot k)+3\\cdot (2\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-2-2k+18 \\\\  & =16-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 16-2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =16 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134553 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134553 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134553 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134553 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186134553 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09585884-a23f-5d81-a9d8-5d380fd1cbe2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134553 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 5+5\\cdot ((-5)\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (k\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =-15-75+10k \\\\  & =-90+10k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ -90+10k & =0 \\\\ 10k & =90 \\\\ k & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134554 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134554 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134554 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134554 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=k{t}^{2}-2t$ and $q(t)=2t+1$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134554 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6628de02-57af-5dca-abf1-406df4d71a7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134554 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(1)q(1)+p(0)q(0)+p(-1)q(-1) \\\\  & =(k-2)(2+1)+(0)(0+1)+(k+2)(-2+1) \\\\  & =(3k-6)+0+(-k-2) \\\\  & =2k-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2k-8 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134556 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134556 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134556 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134556 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[0,2]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=t$ and $y(t)=k-5{t}^{3}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134556 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9f4765c-0e5e-5d52-b8b4-0d6e50464ddb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134556 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}t(k-5{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(kt-5{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}-{t}^{5}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2k-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2k-32 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =32 \\\\ k & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134806 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134806 #edb/ref 1061 31877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134806 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66487d7c-591b-5eac-bee6-8268950dfb3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134806 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134807 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134807 #edb/ref 1061 31878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134807 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d41bed55-deaa-51e5-8029-cb54c1bd8d90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134807 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134808 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134808 #edb/ref 1061 31879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134808 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df499b9c-4c0c-5bb6-956a-e21b99307e64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134808 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134809 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134809 #edb/ref 1061 31880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134809 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc2eefbc-4bae-5404-bedd-abdf5f1e26c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134809 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134810 #edb/ref 1061 31884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134810 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0ffc15f3-7285-538c-8000-b136ed1e04a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134810 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135101 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ be vectors in a vector space $V$ equipped with an inner product $\\langle \\cdot , \\cdot \\rangle.$ \n\nTwo vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ are **orthogonal with respect to the inner product** $\\langle \\cdot , \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$ if their inner product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x} , \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nIf the two vectors are orthogonal, then we denote this by $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}.$ For instance, the vectors \n\n![](images/3d/3d118ca8f8b3ea3e1b978d7bcd0159b1774ea4e8848f39d3a2566e6931b4c2f2.svg)\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^3$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x} , \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = x_1y_1 + 2x_2y_2 + x_3y_3\n$$\n\nare orthogonal, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (1\\cdot (-1))+0\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2-2+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ in a vector space $V$ equipped with an inner product $\\langle \\cdot , \\cdot \\rangle$ is orthogonal to any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ since $\\langle \\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{x} \\rangle = 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135101 #edb/ref 1065 5089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135101 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "77689cf4-cbcb-5c6f-bd2f-c407e888c4cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135101 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175