ef 17592186329137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329526 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329526 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329526 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186329005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329526 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c2a4a86f-1208-5201-973f-39806b593711" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329750 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452538 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329750 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452539 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329750 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452540 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329750 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44058" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329750 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329750 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{2}$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329750 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00ac6a8e-9862-5276-9835-c156c0dea515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329750 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329751 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329751 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329751 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329751 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1]\\,,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=k-2{t}^{3}$ and $y(t)=t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329751 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e996ecb-6acb-5d32-96b4-9b95b6fb1647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329751 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product is equal to zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t(k-2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(kt-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}-\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2}-\\frac{2}{5} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357804 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357804 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357804 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05f101e5-cfde-5208-a26b-ab71b5c23c15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357871 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357871 #edb/ref 108